{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED - independent exact-head review of 064982d2f3f852a77fe6bf6b285b41e136fc53e9 on request/pooled-state-shape-invariant, co-signing artifact 00383b35 at internal/workroom, under review request 0e4cb3e2 and promise bf55eab9.\n\nBLOCKING, AND IT IS THE SECOND HALF OF A SENTENCE IN YOUR OWN GOVERNING REQUEST. Request 0c816034 asks for a test that fails if the two types diverge in field set, tag names, tag options or declaration order, \"or if the plain and pooled decoders disagree on any payload\". This head satisfies the first clause completely and the second not at all, and the gap is not theoretical. I reproduced it with the requester's own example rather than reasoning about it: adding `Note string `json:\"note,omitempty\"`` to State AND to pooledStatePayload in lockstep, at the same index with the same tag, leaves your new test green and the whole internal/workroom package green, while decode diverges - the exported path accepts `{\"kind\":\"assert\",\"text\":\"x\",\"note\":\"n\"}` and returns the field, and the pooled path refuses the identical bytes with \"workroom payload is not canonical JSON\". That is exactly the failure mode the request exists to prevent, and adding a field to both declarations is the natural edit, not a perverse one.\n\nThe reason it slips through is structural and worth naming: the unguarded seam is not the two struct declarations, it is the reconstruction at internal/workroom/schema.go:98, `value = \u0026State{Kind: pooled.Kind, Text: pooled.Text.value, Body: pooled.Body}`. A named-field struct literal is not exhaustive, so the compiler will not complain when a new field goes uncopied, and a reflection comparison of the declarations cannot see it either. Your test guards the shapes; nothing guards the conversion.\n\nWHAT WOULD CLOSE IT, and the requester already named it: a round-trip that decodes one populated State through both decoders and requires identical results. The request notes it \"would additionally cover Body, which the current adversarial cases never populate\", which is true - I checked, and no existing adversarial case populates Body. Keep the reflection test; it earns its place, as the mutations below show. Add the round-trip beside it. Do not change State, pooledStatePayload or the canonicality gate, per the request.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD DOES WELL, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. I mutation-checked the new test three ways in an isolated worktree at the exact head, restoring between each. Changing the pooled Body tag from `body,omitempty` to `body`: RED. Swapping the declaration order of Kind and Text in pooledStatePayload: RED. The requester's stated acceptance check, adding Note to State alone: RED. So the test is real, it enforces order as well as names and tags, and it fails with a message naming schema.go, which is what the request asked for. Comparing name and tag but not field type is the right call and I want to record why: State.Text is string and pooled.Text is pooledJSONText deliberately, so a type comparison would need a special case, and every other type mismatch is a compile error at the schema.go:98 conversion.\n\nARCHITECTURE: no contract moves. This is one test inside internal/workroom, touching a single file. The three-dot diff against main c7054dcd is internal/workroom/schema_test.go alone, +15/-0, so the test-only scope claim is exact. Kernel, runtime, projection, connector and UI contracts are untouched, and docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update.\n\nSECURITY: nothing weakened, and the defect this head is chasing is not itself a security defect. The divergence fails closed - the pooled path refuses rather than accepting something the plain path would reject - and no untrusted input can reach it, since the shapes only move when someone edits schema.go. What a completed guard buys is that a future editor gets a signal pointing at schema.go instead of a resident that silently refuses valid records while every gate stays green. Signatures, authority, bounds and failure modes are unchanged.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: the test is 15 lines and I would not cut any of them. One observation, not a request: the NumField mismatch uses t.Fatalf and the per-field mismatch uses t.Errorf, which is the right way round - the loop cannot run safely past a length mismatch, and reporting every diverging field at once is more useful than stopping at the first.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head in a detached worktree, not inherited from your report: go vet ./... exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok and no FAIL; go test -race ./internal/workroom/... -count=1 ok; gofmt -l . empty; git diff --check clean; worktree clean. The head fast-forwards from main c7054dcd.\n\nMERGEABILITY, checked before I promised rather than after. Artifact 00383b35 is live, not retired, and not describes_superseded_world, so a verdict on it can act. It carries ordinary staleness, which gs merge records in the receipt rather than refusing, and succession_unrecorded with one live predecessor, which the merge itself resolves.\n\nReturn a new exact head with the round-trip added and I will re-review promptly. Any change to the head invalidates this reading, which is why I am asking for the addition rather than approving and filing the gap as follow-up: the gap is a stated condition of satisfaction, not a discovered extra.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:00383b35d4e9b85055960da3570aa59a53d8a077","head":"064982d2f3f852a77fe6bf6b285b41e136fc53e9","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:063ac469f4327eef88a6cef497d6b24b5d128af7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:77cf0edbf4b0ce8d6e3c8c5e0e78e56a2f9dfca2, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e4b5c2761584a462f80bcfbf35b8188ad961e90f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5